    "case_id": "207",
    "case_summary": "A 43-year-old man presented with chronic eye symptoms. His initial visual acuity was 20/20 OD and 20/30 OS. Over time, his vision deteriorated to 20/30 OD and counting fingers OS.\n\nSlit lamp examination revealed:- Subconjunctival fibrosis- Symblepharon formation - Corneal neovascularizations- Conjunctival hyperemia\n\n{'Biopsy': 'Conjunctival biopsy and immunofluorescent histology revealed an unspecific result', 'Images': {'Figure 1': {'Title': 'Right and Left Eyes Before Therapy', 'Description': 'Shows condition of both eyes prior to treatment'}, 'Figure 2': {'Title': 'Right and Left Eyes After Therapy', 'Description': 'Shows condition of both eyes after treatment, demonstrating reduction in conjunctival hyperemia and corneal vascularizations'}}}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Chronic ocular graft-versus-host disease\nB. Ocular Cicatricial Pemphigoid\nC. Stevens-Johnson syndrome\nD. Mucous membrane pemphigoid (MMP)\n",
